Formulas **20.9.1** and **20.9.3** are valid for arbitrary \(a\), \(q\), provided \(r\) is also known; they give multiples of **20.4.12**, normalized so as to approach the corresponding Hankel functions \(H_{1}^{\mbox{\tiny{(ij)}}}(\sqrt{q}e^{i})\), \(H_{2}^{\mbox{\tiny{(ij)}}}(\sqrt{q}e^{i})\), as \(z\rightarrow\infty\). See [20.36], section 2.63. The formula is especially useful if \(\mid\cosh z\mid\)is large and \(q\) is not too large; thus if \(\sigma\)= \(-1\), the absolute ratio of two successive terms in the expansion is essentially

\[\left|\left(\frac{\sqrt{q}}{m}+\frac{m}{4\sqrt{q}}+2\right)\right.\!\!\left( \cosh\;z+1\right)\right|.\]

If \(a\), \(q\), \(z\), \(v\) are real, the real and imaginary components of \(Mc_{r}^{\mbox{\tiny{(ij)}}}(z,\,q)\) are \(Mc_{r}^{\mbox{\tiny{(ij)}}}(z,\,q)\) and \(Mc_{r}^{\mbox{\tiny{(ij)}}}(z,\,q)\), respectively; similarly for the components of \(M_{r}^{\mbox{\tiny{(ij)}}}(z,\,q)\). If the parameters are complex

**20.9.5**: \(Mc_{r}^{\mbox{\tiny{(ij)}}}(z,\,q)\)=\(\frac{1}{2}\left[Mc_{r}^{\mbox{\tiny{(ij)}}}(z,\,q)+Mc_{r}^{\mbox{\tiny{(ij)}}}(z,\,q)\right]\)
**20.9.6**: \(Mc_{r}^{\mbox{\tiny{(ij)}}}(z,\,q)\)=\(-\frac{i}{2}\left[Mc_{r}^{\mbox{\tiny{(ij)}}}(z,\,q)-Mc_{r}^{\mbox{\tiny{(ij)}}}(z,\,q)\right]\)

